Figure - TaskFlow Phase 06 architecture. The browser sees Next.js on port 3000 while the independent NestJS API listens on port 4000.

Book 4 of Phase 06

FULL_STACK_DEVELOPER_2026_COMPLETE - Phase 06                                                                           Browser Fetch and CORS

Second connection: let browser JavaScript call NestJS
Now we intentionally move the request into a Client Component. The browser page is served from port 3000 while the API is
on port 4000. Those are different origins, so the browser begins enforcing Cross-Origin Resource Sharing rules.

Browser Origin                           GET /todos                            API Origin

http://localhost:3000                                                      http://localhost:4000

different port = different origin

Browser checks                                                     Nest CORS config

response CORS headers                                                    allowed origins

If the server does not grant the browser origin, JavaScript cannot read the cross-origin response.

Figure - CORS is permission communicated through HTTP headers and enforced by browsers. It is not a replacement for authentication
or authorization.

What counts as an origin?
Origin mental model

origin = scheme + host + port

http://localhost:3000
http://localhost:4000   <- different port, different origin
http://127.0.0.1:3000 <- different host from localhost

Enable only the frontend origins we need
apps/api/src/main.ts - before app.listen(...)

app.enableCors({
origin: [
'http://localhost:3000',
'http://127.0.0.1:3000',
],
});

NestJS exposes enableCors() on the application object. With the Fastify platform, Nest uses the platform-appropriate CORS
integration under the hood. The important part for the learner is the policy: the API tells browsers which origins may read
cross-origin responses.

CORS IS NOT AUTH

FULL_STACK_DEVELOPER_2026_COMPLETE - Phase 06                                                                             Browser Fetch and CORS

Allowing http://localhost:3000 through CORS does not prove who the user is, does not protect a Todo from another
authenticated user, and does not make the API private. Authentication and authorization arrive later.

