apps/web/src/app/todos-browser/browser-todos.tsx

'use client';

import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';
import { fetchTodos, type Todo } from '@/lib/api/todos';

export function BrowserTodos() {
const [todos, setTodos] = useState<Todo[]>([]);
const [error, setError] = useState<string | null>(null);

useEffect(() => {
const baseUrl = process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL;
if (!baseUrl) {
setError('NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL is not configured');
return;
}

fetchTodos(baseUrl).then(setTodos).catch((err: unknown) => {
setError(err instanceof Error ? err.message : 'Unknown error');
});
}, []);

if (error) return <p role="alert">{error}</p>;
return <ul>{todos.map((todo) => <li key={todo.id}>{todo.title}</li>)}</ul>;
}

EDUCATIONAL SCAFFOLDING

This useEffect data-fetching example is intentionally temporary. Later, TanStack Query will own browser server-state fetching,
caching, retries and mutation synchronization. We use raw fetch now so the network mechanics remain visible.

How to verify CORS instead of guessing
1         Temporarily comment out app.enableCors(...) and restart the API.
2         Open the browser route that performs the client fetch.
3         Use DevTools -> Network and Console. Observe that the API may receive a request while browser JavaScript is
prevented from using a disallowed response.
4         Restore the exact development-origin CORS policy and retry.
5         Confirm the response is readable by the Client Component.

Official source notes
Primary framework references: Next.js App Router Fetching Data and fetch API documentation; Next.js Environment Variables
documentation; NestJS CORS documentation. Browser protocol references: MDN CORS, preflight request, and Fetch API documentation.

The course remains version-locked to the versions established in Phase 00. These sources are used to verify concepts and current API
behavior, not to silently upgrade the course midstream.

